WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:01.049
Film Courage: Mark, your new book (A&nbsp;SCREENWRITER’S
JOURNEY TO SUCCESS: Tips, tricks and tactics

00:00:01.049 --> 00:00:05.400
to survive as a working screenwriter in Hollywood),
I understand from what you told me off camera,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.090
it took you five years to actually write it?

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:15.210
Mark Sanderson: It did. I didn’t sit down
to write a book initially. I was blogging

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:23.480
and I wanted to get my past 20 years of professional
experience out of my mind and heart and try

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:29.669
to be able to share it with people. And so
after five or six years of blogging I realized

00:00:29.669 --> 00:00:37.510
I had enough information to create something.
And so I started the process of stripping

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:43.610
down the articles and refabricating them into
chapters and then I thought “Well, I’m

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:49.320
going to chart my journey from how I started
in the business as a filmmakers at eleven

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:56.879
years old. What was that spark, that creative
spark? And chart it all the way until today.

00:00:56.879 --> 00:01:03.589
Not so much as a memoir but also using examples
and funny anecdotal stories but disciplines

00:01:03.589 --> 00:01:09.120
that I’ve used that have helped me stay
in the game that hopefully other writers will

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.880
be able to see and go “Oh, yeah? I’m not
doing that” or “I need to do that.”

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:19.500
Film Courage: Well you referenced a friend’s
grand father gave you a camera when you were

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.491
both around [age] eleven and that sparked
your love for making movies together. Have

00:01:23.491 --> 00:01:26.120
you ever thought about what if that hadn’t
happened?

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:33.380
Mark Sanderson: Oh, yeah. I’m not sure…I
would have gone into some creative endeavor.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:37.810
I wanted to be a cartoonist originally because
I love to draw and I still do. And in the

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:42.750
book, when I was writing the book, I drank
a lot of coffee and so it would leave a coffee

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:48.470
ring on the notes so I would do little doodles
and I’ve turned those into coffee ring mugs

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:52.611
and TV-shirts and things like that. But they’re
also in the book after every chapter. And

00:01:52.611 --> 00:02:01.780
so I wanted to be a cartoonist or animator
or something creative. My parents eventually

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:12.980
bought me my own camera but it’s interesting
there are these times in your life that you

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:18.870
can only look back on now. I don’t know.
It would be interesting, I would do something

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:24.680
creative, absolutely. I was not going to be
a doctor (not that that was…), I was not

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:29.270
going to have a day job. I was going to do
something creative so I knew that because

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:34.640
my parents were always very supportive of
my brother and myself in the creative arts

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:42.069
and my brother is a graphic designer and he’s
always had the ability and talent for art.

00:02:42.069 --> 00:02:50.200
So my parent, my dad wanted to be a singer
and he came out to Los Angeles from Michigan

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:56.880
in the early ‘60s and he would sing in piano
bars and he recorded little 45 records and

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.100
he wanted to be a singer and that was his
creative thing. But I always saw him doing

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:03.840
poetry and drawing and things like that.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.880
And my mom artistically inclined with jewelry
making. And so I came from creative parents

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:16.190
but they came from an era where their parents
didn’t support them. You were going to get

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:20.600
a job in business or you were going to go
in a factory. It was just very clear cut.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:24.730
And so I think they wanted to break that mold
and really supported my brother and I for

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:30.959
that and that’s so vitally important. In
the book I dedicate the book to my parents

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:37.290
because without them there would be no support
system. You know, that’s vital I think.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.029
Film Courage: That’s beautiful.

